      "writtenOn": "2025-02-10T13:10:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "(@jiyong@google.com, please correct me if I\u0027m wrong)\n\nMy understanding from reading the implementation is that it copies `/data/misc_de/\u003cuser\u003e/apexdata/\u003capex-name\u003e` into `/data/misc_de/\u003cuser\u003e/apexrollback/\u003capex-name\u003e` (for all users and APEXes) so that in case of a rollback, the contents under `/data/misc_de/\u003cuser\u003e/apexdata/\u003capex-name\u003e` can also be restored. Therefore, if `bpfloader` doesn\u0027t use (read or write) `/data/misc_de/\u003cuser\u003e/apexdata/com.android.tethering` or `/data/misc_de/\u003cuser\u003e/apexdata/com.android.uprobestats`, there should be no problem.\n\nThe files under these directories are labeled with [`apex_module_data_file`](https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:system/sepolicy/private/file_contexts;l\u003d780;drc\u003d840b607104c13e51e99edc991cf70cc4a033ea7f). Given that `bpfloader` SELinux context does not seem to be allowed to read or write files/dirs with this label, and the build passses with the following neverallow rules, I believe `bpfloader` doesn\u0027t use these directories.\n```\nneverallow bpfloader apex_module_data_file:file { create_file_perms x_file_perms };\nneverallow bpfloader apex_module_data_file:dir create_dir_perms;\n```\n\n(We could add these `neverallow` rules to bpfloader.te, too)",
